    "The Church of the Savior on Spilled Blood is a cathedral in Saint Petersburg, built on the exact spot where Tsar Alexander II was assassinated on March 1, 1881, by members of the revolutionary Narodnaya Volya ('People's Will') movement who threw a dynamite bomb into his carriage. His son, Tsar Alexander III, commissioned the church as a sacred imperial memorial. Built in vivid contrast to Saint Petersburg's predominant Baroque and Neoclassical architecture, drawing inspiration from medieval Russian churches like Saint Basil's in Moscow.",
