    "The Catherine Palace was the rococo summer residence of the Russian tsars, located in the town of Tsarskoye Selo (Pushkin), 25 kilometers south of Saint Petersburg. Named after Catherine I, wife of Peter the Great. Transformed into an immense palace in 1752 by Empress Elizabeth, who hired court architect Bartolomeo Rastrelli to outshine Versailles. Over 100 kilograms of pure gold leaf was used to gild the exterior statues and roof urns.",
